                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- In our model, risk neutrality is assigned to the market maker's pricing problem, not to the agents bearing fundamental risk. This is the standard locus of risk-neutrality in the microstructure literature (Kyle, 1985; Glosten and Milgrom, 1985): subsequent work relaxes risk-neutrality for the informed trader while continuing to model the market maker as risk-neutral and competitive (Holden and Subrahmanyam, 1994; Baruch, 2002), isolating risk-bearing to the party with fundamental exposure to v rather than to the price-setting mechanism itself.",
            "block_bbox": [
                212,
                226,
                1084,
                371
            ],
            "block_id": 1,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 1,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    212.0,
                    226.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    226.0
                ],
                [
                    1084.0,
                    371.0
                ],
                [
                    212.0,
                    371.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 3. Distributional Assumptions on Value and Noise Trading",
            "block_bbox": [
                163,
                385,
                763,
                410
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    163.0,
                    385.0
                ],
                [
                    763.0,
                    385.0
                ],
                [
                    763.0,
                    410.0
                ],
                [
                    163.0,
                    410.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- We assume the liquidation value is normally distributed,  $ v \\sim N(p_0, \\Sigma_0) $, and that aggregate noise-trader demand is normally distributed,  $ u \\sim N(0, \\sigma_u^2) $, independent of  $ v $. Normality is imposed directly on the asset's terminal value and on noise-trader order flow, which is what permits the linear-equilibrium closed-form solution for  $ \\lambda $ in Proposition 1.",
            "block_bbox": [
                213,
                426,
                1083,
                522
            ],
            "block_id": 3,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 3,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    213.0,
                    426.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    426.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    522.0
                ],
                [
                    213.0,
                    522.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- We treat the informativeness ratio  $ \\sigma_u/\\sqrt{\\Sigma_0} $ as the key state variable governing price impact, in place of the asset volatility  $ \\sigma $ and the asset-to-debt ratio  $ A/F $ in the Merton framework.",
            "block_bbox": [
                212,
                528,
                1083,
                578
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    212.0,
                    528.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    528.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    578.0
                ],
                [
                    212.0,
                    578.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 4. Equity Trading Volume as Observed Order Flow",
            "block_bbox": [
                163,
                592,
                684,
                617
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    163.0,
                    592.0
                ],
                [
                    684.0,
                    592.0
                ],
                [
                    684.0,
                    617.0
                ],
                [
                    163.0,
                    617.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "● We assume that observed equity trading volume (and, in particular, our signed-volume construction, Method 2) is a noisy but informative empirical proxy for the latent order flow  $ y = x + u $ that the market maker actually conditions on.",
            "block_bbox": [
                213,
                632,
                1082,
                705
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    213.0,
                    632.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    632.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    705.0
                ],
                [
                    213.0,
                    705.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "● By treating signed volume as a proxy for y, we assume that the direction of daily price changes carries information about whether volume reflects informed or noise trading. This is consistent with Kyle (1985)'s model, in which only the informed component of flow is correlated with v.",
            "block_bbox": [
                212,
                713,
                1083,
                786
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 8,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    212.0,
                    713.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    713.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    786.0
                ],
                [
                    212.0,
                    786.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- Consistent with informed-trading models more broadly ((Easley et al., 1996a, 1998b)), we posit that if the informed trader anticipates favorable news about v, she will submit larger buy orders, raising both signed order flow and price.",
            "block_bbox": [
                211,
                793,
                1083,
                865
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 9,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    211.0,
                    793.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    793.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    865.0
                ],
                [
                    211.0,
                    865.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 5. Fixed Supply and Single-Period (or Sequential-Auction) Trading",
            "block_bbox": [
                162,
                879,
                848,
                904
            ],
            "block_id": 9,
            "block_order": 10,
            "group_id": 9,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    169.0,
                    879.0
                ],
                [
                    847.0,
                    879.0
                ],
                [
                    847.0,
                    903.0
                ],
                [
                    169.0,
                    903.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "• As in Kyle (1985), we assume a fixed quantity of the risky asset available for trading within each round, with no new issuance or share buybacks occurring within a trading period.",
            "block_bbox": [
                210,
                919,
                1081,
                969
            ],
            "block_id": 10,
            "block_order": 11,
            "group_id": 10,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    210.0,
                    919.0
                ],
                [
                    1081.0,
                    919.0
                ],
                [
                    1081.0,
                    969.0
                ],
                [
                    210.0,
                    969.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- In the dynamic extension (Section 3.3), trading occurs over a finite or continuous sequence of rounds before v is publicly revealed at T; we assume no additional informed traders enter or exit during this window, so that a single informed agent's strategy fully characterizes the informed component of order flow.",
            "block_bbox": [
                211,
                976,
                1084,
                1073
            ],
            "block_id": 11,
            "block_order": 12,
            "group_id": 11,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    211.7706298828125,
                    969.0062255859375
                ],
                [
                    1083.7705078125,
                    976.0062255859375
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1072.0
                ],
                [
                    211.0000762939453,
                    1065.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 6. Information Revelation in Place of Bankruptcy and Recovery",
            "block_bbox": [
                164,
                1086,
                814,
                1111
            ],
            "block_id": 12,
            "block_order": 13,
            "group_id": 12,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    164.0,
                    1086.0
                ],
                [
                    814.0,
                    1086.0
                ],
                [
                    814.0,
                    1111.0
                ],
                [
                    164.0,
                    1111.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "● We lean on Kyle’s information-revelation assumption: the liquidation value v becomes publicly known at a terminal date T, at which point the price converges to value and all private information is impounded. The economic friction is the gradual, imperfect revelation of v through the order-flow process, formalized by the time-varying price-impact coefficient  $ \\lambda(t) $ (Proposition 4).",
            "block_bbox": [
                213,
                1127,
                1082,
                1224
            ],
            "block_id": 13,
            "block_order": 14,
            "group_id": 13,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    213.0,
                    1127.0
                ],
                [
                    1081.0,
                    1127.0
                ],
                [
                    1081.0,
                    1216.0
                ],
                [
                    213.0,
                    1216.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- We assume that the rate of information revelation and the time path of  $ \\lambda(t) $ differentiate short-horizon from long-horizon predictability.",
            "block_bbox": [
                211,
                1230,
                1081,
                1280
            ],
            "block_id": 14,
            "block_order": 15,
            "group_id": 14,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    211.0,
                    1230.0
                ],
